This is the second of two tutorials on `mim`.
link:tutorial-development.asc[Part 1] built a cross-language dice roller in an
environment called `dnd`. This part turns it into something other processes --
and AI assistants -- can call.

[WARNING]
.Experimental Feature
====

Serving works; the *portable image* path (`freeze`/`unfreeze`) is in
development, likely to change, and probably buggy. The `/eval` endpoint is
currently broken on every host (see <<serving-eval>>).
Treat sections <<serving-eval>> and
<<freeze-unfreeze>> as provisional.

====

== Where we left off

Part 1 ended with a `dnd` environment holding a program that spans three
languages, built in your working directory and run from there:

[source, console]
----
$ ./dnd rollAdv
"You rolled a 10"
----

That launcher only works next to its build directory. To make the program a
durable part of the environment -- something the server can find and `freeze`
can export -- it has to be installed.

Three steps stand between a source file and a running service, and they are
separate on purpose: installing a program does not expose it, and exposing it
does not serve it until you ask.

[cols="1,2,3", options="header"]
|===
| Step | Command | What it does

| *install*
| `mim install dnd.loc`
| Builds the program into the environment's permanent state.

| *expose*
| `mim expose add dnd --as mcp,api`
| Records which installed modules answer on which protocol.

| *serve*
| `mim start`
| Launches one HTTP front end over the exposed set.
|===

== Installing the program

[source, console]
----
$ mim install dnd.loc
...
Installed 'dnd' to .../environments/dnd/bin/dnd
----

`mim install` resolves the program's declared dependencies into the environment
*before* building it, then runs `morloc make --install` inside the environment.
Nothing is left in your working directory: the compiled pools, the launcher,
and every file covered by `include` in `package.yaml` go into the environment's
own state.

The installed program is named after its *module* -- the `module <name>`
declaration -- not after the source file. That is different from a plain
`morloc make`, which names the launcher after the source basename, and the
difference is the point: `make` drops a local artifact in your working
directory, the way a C compiler hands you `a.out`, while `--install` writes
into a global namespace where the program's identity is the name other code
imports it by. The entry file is conventionally `main.loc` and carries no
identity at all:

[source, console]
----
$ ls
dice.R  dice.hpp  dice.py  main.loc      # main.loc declares `module dnd`
$ morloc make --install main.loc
...
Installed 'dnd' to /opt/morloc/bin/dnd
$ morloc list --programs
  dnd  2 commands
$ ls
dice.R  dice.hpp  dice.py  main.loc      # unchanged
----

Everything downstream -- `expose`, `start`, `/call/<module>/...`, the MCP tool
names -- addresses the program as `dnd`. Install as many programs into one
environment as you like; each is independently addressable.

`mim install` also accepts a directory:

[source, console]
----
$ mim install ./dnd
----

This routes through `morloc install --build`, which installs the *module* as
well as the program, so other code (and the `/eval` endpoint) can `import dnd`.
It expects `main.loc` as the entry point and it requires the directory basename
to equal the module name:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc install --build .
Fetching module 'dnd2'...
morloc: user error (declared module name 'dnd' in dnd.loc does not match the
install directory name 'dnd2'. Either rename the directory to match the module,
or edit the `module` header so the names agree.)
----

// REVIEWER: real error, wrapped for width. The distinction between the two
// install paths matters more than it looks: `mim install <file>` installs a
// PROGRAM only, so `/eval` cannot `import` it. I hit exactly that while
// testing eval. If that is not the intended split, this section needs to say
// something different.

Reinstalling after an edit needs a little care: `mim install` passes no
`--force`, and `morloc make --install` refuses to overwrite an existing
install. Remove it first, or reach past `mim install` for the run that
overwrites:

[source, console]
----
$ mim run -- morloc uninstall dnd            # then: mim install dnd.loc
$ mim run -- morloc make --install --force dnd.loc   # or overwrite directly
----

// REVIEWER: verify. `Cmd::Install` in main.rs builds the argv without
// `--force`, and installProgram throws "'dnd' is already installed. Use
// --force to overwrite." That looks like a rough edge worth a `mim install
// --force` flag.

== Declaring what is served

`mim expose` edits a per-environment record of *intent*. Nothing is served
until `start` realizes it. The two network views are independent sets:

[source, console]
----
$ mim expose add dnd --as mcp,api
Exposed 'dnd' over mcp, api in 'dnd'. Run 'mim start' to serve.
$ mim expose list
Exposed in 'dnd':
  dnd  [mcp, api]

Run 'mim start' to serve this set.
$ mim expose rm dnd
----

`--as` takes `mcp`, `api`, or both. `mcp` is the Model Context Protocol view --
each exported command becomes a tool an AI assistant can call. `api` is a plain
JSON HTTP view. A module must already be installed; `expose add` checks for the
launcher and refuses a name it cannot find, which catches typos before you get
a confusing 404 at serve time.

There is a third, separate capability -- `eval` -- covered in
<<serving-eval>>.

== Serving

[source, console]
----
$ mim start
Starting serve container morloc-serve-<user>-dnd on ports 9000:9000...
Container morloc-serve-<user>-dnd started
  Logs:   mim logs
  Stop:   mim stop
  Status: mim status
  MCP:  http://127.0.0.1:9000/mcp
  API:  http://127.0.0.1:9000/call/<module>/<command>
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dnd": {
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:9000/mcp"
    }
  }
}
----

// REVIEWER: source-derived (serve.rs:serve_environment + the Cmd::Start
// handler + print_http_mcp_config). On the native backend the first three
// lines are instead "Serving 'dnd' on 127.0.0.1:9000 (native, host-local)."
// plus a note that a native serve is unsupervised. I show the container form
// because that is the one people will deploy.

One process, one listener, both adapters. On the container backends `start`
bind-mounts the environment's state into a *read-only* container running
`morloc-nexus router`; on the native backend it spawns the same nexus as a
detached host process. Either way there is no build step -- the nexus reads the
programs you installed.

The default port is 9000 when anything is served over MCP and 8080 otherwise;
if that port is busy `mim` picks the next free one and says so. Pin it with
`-p HOST:CONTAINER`.

The `mcpServers` block goes to *stdout* as pure JSON while the human-facing
lines go to stderr, so `mim start > dnd.mcp.json` gives you a client
configuration ready to drop into an assistant.

=== The endpoints

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Route | Purpose

| `GET /health`
| Liveness. Answered before the auth gate, so a health check needs no token.

| `GET /discover`
| The API index: which modules answer, and where to read their signatures.

| `GET /discover/<module>`
| Machine-readable help for one module: commands, ordered arguments, types.

| `POST /call/<module>/<command>`
| Call a command. Body is a positional JSON array, or `{"args": [...]}`.

| `POST /mcp`
| MCP over HTTP (JSON-RPC). Session-based; see below.

| `POST /eval`
| Sandboxed expression evaluation, when the capability is enabled.
|===

Health and discovery:

[source, console]
----
$ curl -s localhost:9000/health
{"status":"ok"}
----

[source, console]
----
$ curl -s localhost:9000/discover
----

[source, json]
----
{
  "api": {
    "modules": [
      { "module": "dnd", "call": "/call/dnd/<command>", "help": "/discover/dnd" }
    ],
    "call": "/call/<module>/<command>",
    "note": "POST positional args as a JSON array (or {\"args\":[...]}). GET /discover/<module> for each command's ordered arguments."
  },
  "mcp": {
    "endpoint": "/mcp",
    "tools": 2,
    "note": "Use the JSON-RPC `tools/list` method for the MCP tool catalog (tools are named <module>__<command> and take named arguments)."
  },
  "eval": { "enabled": false, "endpoints": ["/eval", "mcp tool 'eval'"] }
}
----

Calling a command. Arguments are positional, in signature order:

[source, console]
----
$ curl -s -X POST localhost:9000/call/dnd/rollAdv -d '[]'
{"status":"ok","result":"You rolled a 18"}
$ curl -s -X POST localhost:9000/call/dnd/roll -d '[3,6]'
{"status":"ok","result":[1,6,2]}
----

Errors come back as `{"status":"error","error":"..."}` with a 5xx status. A
command whose return type carries a `@mime` annotation is the exception: it
returns the raw bytes with the declared `Content-Type` rather than a JSON
envelope, so an endpoint returning a PNG returns an actual PNG.

`GET /discover/<module>` is the same information the CLI's `-h` renders, in
JSON: every command with its ordered arguments, each argument's docstring, its
Morloc type, its wire tag, and a JSON-Schema-shaped `structure`. That is what a
client generates a form or a tool schema from.

=== The MCP view

The MCP adapter speaks JSON-RPC over `POST /mcp` and is session-based: an
`initialize` call returns an `Mcp-Session-Id` header, which every later request
must carry, and `tools/list` is refused until you send the
`notifications/initialized` notification. Real clients do all of this for you;
by hand it looks like this:

[source, console]
----
$ curl -s -D- -X POST localhost:9000/mcp -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize",
         "params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},
                   "clientInfo":{"name":"curl","version":"1"}}}'
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
Mcp-Session-Id: 18107bdea6689f2da7dcc168054abaeb
...

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18",
 "capabilities":{"tools":{"listChanged":false}},
 "serverInfo":{"name":"morloc","version":"0.100.2"}}}
----

Once initialized, the exported commands appear as tools named
`<module>__<command>`, with schemas built from your docstrings and types:

[source, json]
----
{
  "tools": [
    {
      "name": "dnd__roll",
      "description": "Roll n dice with d pips each",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "_1": { "type": "integer", "description": "number of dice" },
          "_2": { "type": "integer", "description": "pips per die" }
        },
        "required": ["_1", "_2"],
        "additionalProperties": false
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "dnd__rollAdv",
      "description": "Roll 2d20 and keep the higher die",
      "inputSchema": { "type": "object", "properties": {}, "required": [],
                       "additionalProperties": false }
    }
  ]
}
----

Note the argument keys: MCP tools take *named* arguments, and unnamed
positional parameters are exposed as `_1`, `_2`, and so on -- not `arg0`, which
is what `/discover` calls them. Calling one:

[source, console]
----
$ curl -s -X POST localhost:9000/mcp -H "Mcp-Session-Id: $SID" \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"tools/call",
         "params":{"name":"dnd__roll","arguments":{"_1":2,"_2":20}}}'
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"[2,15]"}],
 "isError":false}}
----

// REVIEWER: the `_1`/`_2` versus `arg0` split is a genuine trap -- I hit it
// (`unknown argument 'arg0'`) writing this. If naming the arguments in the
// signature fixes it, that is worth saying here; I did not test that.

=== Who can reach it

By default the listener binds the host's `127.0.0.1`, reachable only from the
host, and requires no token. Anything wider is a deliberate opt-in:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Flag | Effect

| `--auth-token <tok>` / `MORLOC_MCP_TOKEN`
| Require `Authorization: Bearer <tok>` on `/mcp` and `/call`. `/health` stays
  open. The environment variable keeps the token off your command line.

| `--expose`
| Bind `0.0.0.0` instead of loopback. Requires a token and `--allow-plaintext`.

| `--allow-plaintext`
| Acknowledge that there is no TLS: token and traffic travel in the clear.

| `--allow-no-auth`
| Permit an off-box endpoint with no token. An open server; strongly
  discouraged.

| `--unsafe`
| On a VM-backed engine (Docker Desktop, podman machine), where a loopback bind
  cannot be confined to the host, serve unauthenticated anyway. Trusted hosts
  only.
|===

The refusals are helpful rather than terse -- attempting `--expose` without
`--allow-plaintext` suggests an SSH tunnel instead:

[source, console]
----
$ ssh -N -L 9000:127.0.0.1:9000 <host>
----

which is the right answer most of the time.

== Watching and stopping

[source, console]
----
$ mim status
Running servers:
  ENV              MODE         MODULES              URL                              STATUS
  dnd              mcp+api      dnd                  http://127.0.0.1:9000            Up 5 seconds

$ mim logs          # add -f to follow
$ mim stop
Stopped serving environment: dnd
----

// REVIEWER: source-derived (the Cmd::Status printer and Cmd::Stop in main.rs).
// Column widths are exact; the values are illustrative.

`status` reads a runtime record written by `start`, so it reports the mode,
served modules, and URL correctly even under host networking where the engine
itself shows no published port. `logs` prints the router's captured per-daemon
stderr first -- which is where a pool that died at startup leaves its
complaint -- and then the container's own log stream. `start` and `stop` are
symmetric; `start --force` replaces a live serve rather than refusing.

[[serving-eval]]
== Composing new expressions with eval

[WARNING]
.Broken today
====

`/eval`, `mim eval`, and the MCP `eval` tool all fail with `morloc: failed to
create OS thread: Cannot allocate memory` on every host, for every expression.
The nexus caps the forked compiler's address space at 2 GiB (`RLIMIT_AS`),
which the GHC-built `morloc` binary cannot start under. Filed as report 0003 in
the docs repository. The description below is what the feature is meant to do.

====

// REVIEWER: reproduce in one line, no server needed:
//     bash -c 'ulimit -v 2097152; morloc --version'
//   -> morloc: failed to create OS thread: Cannot allocate memory
// The limit is set in data/rust/morloc-nexus/src/mcp.rs around line 1879.

A conventional API serves a fixed set of endpoints. Because Morloc's type
system spans every installed language, a served environment can go further and
accept *new* compositions, type-checked before they run:

[source, console]
----
$ mim expose eval --allow dnd
Enabled eval in 'dnd', allow-list: dnd.
$ mim start
$ mim eval -p 9000 'import dnd (roll); roll 2 20'
----

Served eval is always sandboxed: directly-written IO intrinsics are banned, and
top-level imports are restricted to the allow-list. An empty allow-list
(`mim expose eval` with no `--allow`) permits only module-free expressions. The
unrestricted evaluator is the local `morloc eval` CLI, never a served endpoint.

Two things to know before you reach for it. `mim eval` defaults to port 8080
while an MCP-serving environment defaults to 9000, so pass `-p` unless you
pinned the port. And the allow-list names *modules*, which is a different thing
from the programs you installed: `mim install dnd.loc` installs a program only,
and eval cannot import it. Install the directory (`mim install ./dnd`) if you
want eval to reach the module.

[[freeze-unfreeze]]
== Portable images: freeze and unfreeze

[WARNING]
.Experimental Feature
====

`freeze` and `unfreeze` are in development and likely to change. Neither works
on the native backend -- `freeze` refuses one outright, and
`unfreeze --engine none` is unimplemented -- and `unfreeze` under Apptainer is
unimplemented. Freeze and unfreeze a Docker or Podman environment.

====

`start` serves an environment in place. To move it to another machine, a
registry, or a Kubernetes cluster, export it:

[source, console]
----
$ mim freeze -o ./dnd-freeze
Detecting morloc version from image...
Validating installed programs...
  [ok] dnd (2 commands)
Freezing installed state from .../environments/dnd...
Created ./dnd-freeze/state.tar.gz
Wrote ./dnd-freeze/freeze-manifest.json
Frozen state written to ./dnd-freeze
----

Before writing anything, `freeze` runs each installed program's `--help` in a
throwaway container. A program that cannot start there aborts the freeze,
rather than producing a tarball that fails on the deployment host. On success
you get two files:

`state.tar.gz`:: the environment's `lib/`, `fdb/`, `bin/`, `exe/`, `opt/`, and
  `src/` directories -- everything needed to run, and nothing needed to build.
`freeze-manifest.json`:: an auditable record: Morloc version, timestamp,
  installed modules with SHA-256 checksums, compiled programs with their
  exported commands, the base image, and the Dockerfile layer if there is one.

`unfreeze` builds a standalone image from that pair and validates the result:

[source, console]
----
$ mim unfreeze --from ./dnd-freeze/state.tar.gz --tag dnd-serve:v1
Using base image: ...
Extracting frozen state...
Built serve image: dnd-serve:v1
Validating installed programs...
  [ok] dnd (2 commands)
----

Both files must sit in the same directory -- `unfreeze` reads the manifest to
learn the Morloc version and base image, which is what lets it run on a
deployment host that has no Morloc environments at all. If post-build
validation fails the image is kept for debugging but `unfreeze` exits non-zero,
so CI notices.

The result contains the nexus, the Morloc compiler (the `/eval` endpoint forks
it), the language runtimes, and the frozen state. It needs no `mim`:

[source, console]
----
$ podman run -d -p 8080:8080 dnd-serve:v1
----

`freeze` refuses a dev environment (`--dev`) and a `--local-runtime`
environment, because neither pins a released compiler and so neither is
reproducible. It warns, but proceeds, for an environment with `--mount-home`:
the mount is a run-time bind, not an image layer, so anything the program needs
from that directory has to be baked in or supplied where the image runs.

// REVIEWER: I could not run any of this -- no container engine here. The
// transcripts are from freeze.rs / serve.rs format strings, with the
// churning lines (paths, image tags, per-program [ok] lines) elided. See the
// checklist at the end for what I would test first.

== Sharing an environment between users

A *system-scope* environment lives outside any one user's home and is shared
across the machine. Root creates and maintains it; regular users compile and
run against it.

[source, console]
----
# as root
$ sudo mim new shared --system --engine docker
$ sudo mim modify --env shared --set-default --system

# each user, once
$ sudo usermod -aG docker <username>
----

System environments are stored under `/etc/morloc/environments` (config) and
`/usr/local/share/morloc/environments` (data), and appear in `mim ls` alongside
local ones. Read-only subcommands take `--system` for discovery, so a
non-root user can run `mim ls --system` and `mim info shared --system` without
privileges; mutating subcommands refuse and tell you to re-run under `sudo`.

The system default serves users who have *no local default*. A user's own local
default always wins, so someone who only ever uses `shared` needs to configure
nothing.

Two operational notes. Use Docker rather than Podman for system environments:
Podman stores images per user, so root's images are invisible to everyone else,
whereas Docker's socket model shares them. And the environment's data directory
is read-only to regular users, so anything that writes to it -- `mim install`,
`morloc install` -- has to be run by root. A plain `morloc make` still works for
everyone, because it writes to the working directory. Users who need to install
their own modules should create a local environment alongside the system one.

// REVIEWER: I could not test any of the system-scope path. The claims above
// come from the `--system` handling in main.rs (check_system_write_access,
// find_env_scope, resolve_default_environment) and match what
// utilities-mim.asc documents. The "root must run morloc install" claim is
// the one I would most like confirmed.

== Scripting

`mim` splits its streams so that query output can be captured:

* *Query* subcommands -- `ls`, `info`, `status`, `doctor`, `expose list`,
  `eval` -- write their result to *stdout*. That is the command's return value:
  pipe it, redirect it, parse it.
* *Mutating* subcommands -- `new`, `update`, `modify`, `start`, `stop`,
  `freeze`, `rm`, `nuke` -- write progress and confirmations to *stderr*.
* Errors and warnings always go to stderr.

The one deliberate exception is `mim start`, whose `mcpServers` block is a
return value and goes to stdout while its status lines go to stderr.

[source, console]
----
$ mim start > dnd.mcp.json          # capture the config, watch the status
$ mim update --env dnd 2>/dev/null  # silent update
----

Add `--json` to any query subcommand for a machine-readable form, and use
`doctor`'s exit code as a gate:

[source, console]
----
$ mim doctor --env dnd --strict --json > health.json || exit 1
----

== What to check when you run this

// REVIEWER: this section is written for you, and I would delete it before
// publishing. It is the honest list of what I could not verify.

Ordered by how likely I think each is to bite:

. *`mim install` on an already-installed program.* The argv it builds carries
  no `--force`, and `installProgram` throws on an existing `bin/` entry. If
  that is right, re-installing after an edit is awkward and probably wants a
  `mim install --force`.
. *`/eval` at all.* Report 0003. Fix the `RLIMIT_AS` cap first; nothing in the
  eval story is testable until then.
. *The program/module install split.* `mim install <file>` installs a program;
  `mim install <dir>` installs both. Only the second is importable by eval. If
  that is intentional it needs saying in `mim install -h`; if not, this section
  is wrong.
. *`freeze` -> `unfreeze` -> `podman run` end to end,* on a fresh machine with
  no Morloc environments. That is the path the manifest-reading code exists
  for, and it is the one most likely to have rotted.
. *Port defaults.* `start` picks 9000 when anything is MCP-exposed and 8080
  otherwise, but `mim eval` hardcodes a default of 8080. Anyone following this
  tutorial in order hits that mismatch.
. *`mim status` under Apptainer.* `Cmd::Status` probes podman, docker, and
  apptainer unconditionally and swallows failures; worth confirming it does not
  print a spurious row or a stray engine error on a host with only one of them.
. *The MCP argument names* (`_1`/`_2` from `tools/list` versus `arg0` from
  `/discover`). Both are real; I do not know whether that is intended.

// REVIEWER: two bug reports came out of writing these tutorials, both filed
// in morloc-project.github.io/reports/:
//   0003 - served /eval sets RLIMIT_AS=2GiB, which the GHC-built morloc
//          cannot start under (nexus; blocks the whole eval feature)
//   0004 - adding a package.yaml silently drops `std` from the emitted
//          envspec (compiler; inert today, latent once the solver reads it)
// I also confirmed the open question in report 0001: `morloc make` names the
// launcher after the source file, `morloc make --install` installs under the
// module name. Addendum appended to that report.

